9dexit code). By convention (B[0;1m[36m`0`[39m(B[m means success and any non-zero value means[40dfailure. The (B[0;1m[36m`exit 1`[39m(B[m in the given script sets it explicitly; a run that misses[41d42 falls through to the final (B[0;1m[36m`echo`[39m(B[m and exits (B[0;1m[36m`0`[39m(B[m.[42d[K[43dThe previous command's exit status is readable from the special variable (B[0;1m[36m`$?`[39m(B[m.[44dIt does not appear in the final script because (B[0;1m[36m`while CMD`[39m(B[m tests that status[45ddirectly: the loop reads it for you. (B[0;1m[36m`until CMD`[39m(B[m is the exact inverse and would[46dhave been the other valid skeleton here.[47d[K[48d(B[0;1m[33m### 1. Loop structure and the logic inversion[49d[39m(B[m[K[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[28d[?25l[?12l[?25h[36d[?25l[?12l[?25h[38d[?25l[?12l[?25h[43d[?25l[?12l[?25h[48d[?25l[2;50r[50;1H[24S[1;52r[26;1H(B[0;1m[36m`while command`[39m(B[m means the condition for the body of the loop to run is that[27d(B[0;1m[36m`command`[39m(B[m runs successfully without erroring. So in this case, if the command[28derrors we never enter the body of the loop. Failure-handling code cannot go[29dthere, because it would never run after a failure.[31dThe corollary is that reaching the line [32m*after*[39m(B[m the loop is itself the signal[32dthat the script failed. That is where the reporting belongs.[34d(B[0;1m[33m### 2. The counter and the off-by-one[36d[39m(B[mThe counter is straightforward and I confused myself here. The idea is to make[37dsure the run counter aligns with the actual number of runs done, keeping in mind[38dthat (B[0;1m[36m`i`[39m(B[m only increases by 1 following a [32m*successful*[39m(B[m run.[40dBecause the increment sits in the loop body, and the body only executes on[41dsuccess, the counter structurally counts successful runs and never counts the[42dfailing one. Starting at (B[0;1m[36m`i=1`[39m(B[m folds the failing run into the initial value, so a[43dsingle in-body increment gives the right total with no post-loop patch.[45dBash assignment does no arithmetic: (B[0;1m[36m`i=i+1`[39m(B[m assigns the literal string (B[0;1m[36m`i+1`[39m(B[m,[46dsince a bare word is not a variable reference. (B[0;1m[36m`$(( ))`[39m(B[m is **arithmetic[47dexpansion(B[0;1m[32m**, evaluating to a value you can use; [36m`(( ))`[32m is **[39m(B[marithmetic[48devaluation** as a statement. Inside either, variables are named without (B[0;1m[36m`$`[39m(B[m.[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[31d[?25l[?12l[?25h[34d[?25l[?12l[?25h[36d[?25l[?12l[?25h[40d[?25l[?12l[?25h[45d[?25l[2;50r[50;1H[24S[1;52r[26;1H(B[0;1m[33m### 3. One run per iteration[28d[39m(B[mThis one is clear to me conceptually. I simply had no idea where to put the[29dredirection in the context of a command used as the condition for a while loop.[30dMy wrong version called (B[0;1m[36m`./script.sh`[39m(B[m three times per iteration: once in the[31d(B[0;1m[36m`while`[39m(B[m condition, then twice more inside the body to capture each stream.[33dObviously if we run it more than once per loop, the issue is that we are not[34dredirecting the stdout and stderr of the command run in the while condition.[35dInstead we wipe that away and replace it with the outputs of the commands run[36dinside the loop, which may very well fail even when the one in the condition ran[37dsuccessfully. Each call rolls its own independent random (B[0;1m[36m`n`[39m(B[m, so the run being[38dtested and the runs being captured are unrelated.[40dOf course, this is not even the biggest issue here, which is that since the[41dcondition for the while loop is the script not failing, when the script[42deventually fails we don't even enter the body of the loop, so those additional[43dcalls and redirections never run. On the one iteration whose output actually[44dmatters, the capture never happens at all, and the files are left holding output[45dfrom some earlier passing run.[47dThe fix is that the tested run and the captured run must be the same single run.[48dBoth redirections attach to the (B[0;1m[36m`./script.sh`[39m(B[m on the (B[0;1m[36m`while`[39m(B[m line.[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[28d[?25l[?12l[?25h[33d[?25l[?12l[?25h[40d[?25l[?12l[?25h[47d[?25l[2;50r[50;1H[24S[1;52r[26;1H(B[0;1m[33m### 4. Truncation versus appending[28d[39m(B[mEssentially, if we append with (B[0;1m[36m`>>`[39m(B[m, our text files will contain a wall of[29dmessages from successful runs with the failure message buried somewhere in[30dthere. We would much rather only have the failure message in the log, so (B[0;1m[36m`>`[39m(B[m is[31dthe right choice, since it wipes the file clean instead of appending to its[32dcontents. After the failed run we escape the loop, so (B[0;1m[36m`>`[39m(B[m doesn't run again and[33dwe keep the message we want.[35dThis works because of where the redirection binds. A redirection attaches to the[36dcommand it sits inside, and (B[0;1m[36m`while ... done`[39m(B[m is a (B[0;1m[32m**compound command**[39m(B[m:[38d(B[0;1m[35m- [36m`while ./script.sh > f`[39m(B[m binds to the script, on every iteration. The file is[39;3Hreopened and truncated each time, so it ends holding only the last run.[40d(B[0;1m[35m- [36m`done > f`[39m(B[m binds to the loop as a single unit. The file is opened once and[41;3Hevery iteration's output accumulates in it.[43dNear-identical syntax, opposite behaviour. The per-run form is what makes the[44dtruncation trick work.[46d(B[0;1m[33m### 5. Two streams, two files[48d[39m(B[mThis one is also clear to me, I am simply not familiar with bash syntax. As I[49dunderstand it, it is okay to do here since those are two separate channels that[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[28d[?25l[?12l[?25h[35d[?25l[?12l[?25h[38d[?25l[?12l[?25h[43d[?25l[?12l[?25h[46d[?25l[?12l[?25h[48d[?25l[2dEssentially, if we append with (B[0;1m[36m`>>`[39m(B[m, our text files will contain a wall of[3dmessages from successful runs with the failure message buried somewhere in[4dthere. We would much rather only have the failure message in the log, so (B[0;1m[36m`>`[39m(B[m is[5dthe right choice, since it wipes the file clean instead of appending to its[K[6dcontents. After the failed run we escape the loop, so (B[0;1m[36m`>`[39m(B[m doesn't run again and[7dwe keep the message we want.[K[9dThis works because of where the redirection binds. A redirection attaches to the[10dcommand it sits inside, and (B[0;1m[36m`while ... done`[39m(B[m is a (B[0;1m[32m**compound command**[39m(B[m:[K[11d[K[12d(B[0;1m[35m- [36m`while ./script.sh > f`[39m(B[m binds to the script, on every iteration. The file is[K[13d  reopened and truncated each time, so it ends holding only the last run.[K[14d(B[0;1m[35m- [36m`done > f`[39m(B[m binds to the loop as a single unit. The file is opened once and[15;3Hevery iteration's output accumulates in it.[16d[K[17dNear-identical syntax, opposite behaviour. The per-run form is what makes the[18dtruncation trick work.[K[19d[K[20d(B[0;1m[33m### 5. Two streams, two files[39m(B[m[K[21d[K[22dThis one is also clear to me, I am simply not familiar with bash syntax. As I[23dunderstand it, it is okay to do here since those are two separate channels that[K[24ddo not interact.[K[26dA process gets three numbered channels at startup: (B[0;1m[36m`0`[39m(B[m stdin, (B[0;1m[36m`1`[39m(B[m stdout, (B[0;1m[36m`2`[27d[39m(B[mstderr. The number is the (B[0;1m[32m**file descriptor**[39m(B[m, an integer index into the[28dkernel's table of open files for that process. (B[0;1m[36m`>`[39m(B[m is shorthand for (B[0;1m[36m`1>`[39m(B[m.[K[29d[K[30dSo (B[0;1m[36m`> stdout.txt 2> stderr.txt`[39m(B[m attaches fd 1 and fd 2 to different files on the[31dsame command, and they cannot collide because they are separate entries in that[32dtable. This is the same "everything is a file" idea from lecture 1's (B[0;1m[36m`/sys`[39m(B[m[K[33dexercise.[K[35d(B[0;1m[33m### 6. Two bugs debugged[39m(B[m[K[36d[K[37d(B[0;1m[33m#### The invisible character[38d[39m(B[m[K[39dThe script refused to parse:[K[40d[K[41d(B[0;1m[36m```[39m(B[m[K[42d(B[0;1m[36mbash: exercise3.sh: line 5: syntax error near unexpected token `do'[43d```[39m(B[m[K[44d[K[45dThe (B[0;1m[36m`do`[39m(B[m is flagged as unexpected because the (B[0;1m[36m`while`[39m(B[m line above it did not[46dparse as a valid loop header. The "fake" space appeared, I think, because I[47daccidentally pressed a command in the nano editor. To diagnose it we used[48d(B[0;1m[36m`cat -A`[39m(B[m, which prints non-visible characters and made sure of which exact space[49dcharacters we were using. Afterwards I edited the code manually.[K[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[30d[?25l[?12l[?25h[35d[?25l[?12l[?25h[37d[?25l[?12l[?25h[39d[?25l[?12l[?25h[41d[?25l[?12l[?25h[45d[?25l[2;50r[50;1H[25S[1;52r[26;1H(B[0;1m[36m```[27dwhileM-BM-7./script.sh > stdout.txt 2> stderr.txt$[28d```[30d`cat -A`[39m(B[m renders otherwise invisible bytes: (B[0;1m[36m`$`[39m(B[m at line end, (B[0;1m[36m`^I`[39m(B[m for a tab, and[31d(B[0;1m[36m`M-`[39m(B[m notation for any byte above 127. (B[0;1m[36m`M-BM-7`[39m(B[m is how it shows the two UTF-8[32dbytes (B[0;1m[36m`\xc2\xb7`[39m(B[m, which is (B[0;1m[32m**U+00B7, the middle dot**[39m(B[m. Bash does not treat it as[33da word separator, so (B[0;1m[36m`while·./script.sh`[39m(B[m read as one mashed token.[35dWorth recording: it was not a non-breaking space, and nano had been rendering it[36dliterally as (B[0;1m[36m`·`[39m(B[m the entire time. We both read those dots as nano's own[37dwhitespace display and missed it for several turns. The editor was showing the[38dtruth and we had a prior belief about what the display meant. The general[39dtechnique: when a file behaves differently from how it reads, inspect the bytes[40drather than the rendering.[42d(B[0;1m[33m#### Permission denied[44d[36m```[45dThe script errored during run number 1[46d--- stdout ---[47d--- stderr ---[48dexercise3.sh: line 4: ./script.sh: Permission denied[49d```[26d[39m(B[m[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[30d[?25l[?12l[?25h[35d[?25l[?12l[?25h[42d[?25l[?12l[?25h[44d[?25l[2;50r[50;1H[25S[1;52r[26;1HThree details in that output point the same way. The count is 1, so the loop[27dexited on its first iteration. Both capture files are empty, so the script wrote[28dnothing to either stream. And the error line is prefixed (B[0;1m[36m`exercise3.sh: line 4:`[39m(B[m,[29dwhich means bash itself emitted it rather than (B[0;1m[36m`script.sh`[39m(B[m. Together these say[30dthe file was never executed at all: (B[0;1m[36m`n`[39m(B[m was never rolled, and the loop exited[31dbecause the [32m*attempt*[39m(B[m to run the file returned non-zero.[33dThat reading depended on the counter already being correct. A count of 1 with[34dempty capture files is only a usable signature if the count can be trusted, which[35dis what fixing the off-by-one in section 2 bought.[37dThis is a straightforward case of the user not having permission to execute,[38dwhich I fixed with (B[0;1m[36m`chmod u+x script.sh`[39m(B[m. Alternatively, we could have used[39d(B[0;1m[36m`bash script.sh`[39m(B[m inside the code to avoid the issue entirely.[41dThose two options are the lecture 1 distinction again. (B[0;1m[36m`./script.sh`[39m(B[m is **direct[42dexecution**: it asks the kernel to execute the file, which requires the execute[43dbit and a valid shebang. (B[0;1m[36m`bash script.sh`[39m(B[m is (B[0;1m[32m**interpreter execution**[39m(B[m: it runs a[44dprogram we already have permission to execute, which needs only read permission[45don the script, since the file is opened as data. Identical to (B[0;1m[36m`./semester`[39m(B[m versus[46d(B[0;1m[36m`sh semester`[39m(B[m in lecture 1 exercises 6 and 7.[48d(B[0;1m[36m`chmod u+x`[39m(B[m is the better choice for something invoked repeatedly, rather than[49dprefixing every call site.[26d[?12l[?25h[?25l[?12l[?25h[33d[?25l[?12l[?